course: dictionary
lesson: 4069
title: What is geography?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Geography is a school subject, and it is also a word for how a place is laid out. Geography is about the Earth: its places, countries, land, weather, rivers and people, and how they live.
explain: Geography can mean the layout of a place. «I don't know the geography of this town» means you do not know where things are in it, such as the roads, shops and parks.
explain: Geography lessons use maps, continents, climates, rivers, mountains and cities. Physical geography looks at land and weather, and human geography looks at people, towns and jobs.
fact: Geography comes from a word that means describing the Earth.
fact: Geography is a school subject about places, land, weather, rivers and people.
fact: Physical geography is about land and weather.
fact: Human geography is about people, towns and jobs.
fact: When you ask about the geography of a place, you are asking where things are in it.
exercise: «The Mock Turtle's subjects are sound-alike jokes: Mystery (history), Seaography (geography).» Which school subject is the joke word Seaography copying? | History | *Geography | Latin | Drawing
exercise: «London is the capital of Paris.» Alice's answer is wrong. What is the mistake? | It is a maths mistake about times tables | *It mixes up two places: Paris is the capital of France, and London is in England | It is a mistake about the weather | It is correct, so there is no mistake
exercise: Which of these would you learn about in a geography lesson? | Tens and units | Dance steps | Story writing | *Rivers and mountains
test: Physical geography is mostly about which of these? | Music and singing | Books and poems | Sums and spelling | *Land and weather
test: Human geography is mostly about which of these? | *People, towns and jobs | Animals with shells | Numbers and shapes | Paint and brushes
exam: Which sentence uses geography to mean the layout of a place? | «Four times five is twelve» | «The geography cone snail has a harpoon tooth» | *«I don't know the geography of this town» | «I have geography homework tonight»
